 Each round the user answers reshapes the tree: settled decisions push the frontier outward and unblock questions that depended on them. Recompute the frontier and ask the next round. A question whose answer depends on another question still open in this round belongs to a _later_ round, not this one.
 
-Finding _facts_ is your job, never the user's. When a frontier question needs a fact from the environment (filesystem, tools, etc.), dispatch a sub-agent with a fresh context window to find it; don't ask the user for anything you could look up yourself. Don't block on it: a running exploration is an unsettled prerequisite, so only the questions downstream of it wait for the sub-agent to report; ask the rest of the frontier now. The _decisions_ are the user's: put each to them and wait.
+Finding _facts_ is your job, never the user's. When a frontier question needs a fact from the environment (filesystem, tools, etc.), look it up yourself with your own tools, reaching for a sub-agent with a fresh context window only when the sweep is broad; the user answers nothing you could look up. Don't block on it: a running exploration is an unsettled prerequisite, so only the questions downstream of it wait for the fact; ask the rest of the frontier now. The _decisions_ are the user's: put each to them and wait.
+
+A recommended answer that changes existing behaviour cites the decision record governing that behaviour (the repo's ADRs, its glossary's `_Avoid_` terms, the topic's contract), looked up before you recommend. When the recommendation departs from an accepted decision, the question names that decision and says it supersedes it, so the user decides the departure knowingly. Before recommending a departure, also find the decisions that build on the one you would supersede and the open tracker items citing either (a later decision may already carry it into delivery); the question names each such item and what happens to it.
+
+Before round 1, list the tracker's open Issues whose title or body names the topic's files or skills. An open Issue outranks a doc describing current behaviour: a recommendation overlapping one names that Issue and how the two fit (build on it, wait for it, or drop yours).
 
 The session is done when the frontier is empty: every branch of the design tree visited, nothing left silently assumed. Do not act on it until the user confirms you have reached a shared understanding.
